 No, in C#, when you create an `HttpWebRequest` instance, you are actually creating an object behind the scenes to manage HTTP communication for that specific instance. The port that the `HttpWebRequest` uses is not fixed to the same port across all instances. When you make HTTP requests, the server routing the request will determine the appropriate port if it's required, based on the URL and the server's configuration.

For example, if you're accessing the root path (`http://example.com`), the `HttpWebRequest` object will typically use the default HTTP port (typically port 80) and no additional port is specified in the URL. However, if you are accessing a path that uses a custom port (like `http://example.com:8080`), you need to specify that port in the URL, and the HTTP request will use port 8080 instead.

Here's a basic example of how you might set up an `HttpWebRequest` and specify the URL and port:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Net;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string url = "http://example.com:80"; // Replace with your actual URL and port if different from default HTTP port

        HttpWebRequest request = (HttpWebRequest)WebRequest.Create(url);

        // ... perform additional operations like setting request method, headers, etc.

        using (HttpWebResponse response = (HttpWebResponse)request.GetResponse())
        {
            // Check response status code
            Console.WriteLine("Response status code: " + response.StatusCode);

            // Read response streams like byte array, Text, or HTML (depending on content type)
            // ...
        }
    }
}
```

Important note: Always make sure you have a valid URL and port. Using different ports is commonly done for specific services on the HTTP server that might listen on ports other than the default HTTP port.

 In C#, when you're using `HttpWebRequest` to send HTTP requests, setting the `KeepAlive` property to `true` can help you maintain a persistent connection between your client and the server. This is also known as HTTP connection pooling or HTTP pipelining.

When `KeepAlive` is `true`, the HTTP connection between your client and server remains open after the response to the initial request has been received. This can reduce the overhead of establishing a new connection for each request-response cycle, as the connection is already established and available. This is particularly beneficial when making multiple requests to the same server because the cost of creating a new connection is saved with each subsequent request.

Additionally, with the `KeepAlive` property set to `true`, you can potentially use the same connection for sending multiple requests concurrently (HTTP pipelining), which can further improve performance, especially for high-latency connections. However, keep in mind that not all servers support HTTP pipelining, and even when they do, the precise behavior may vary.

Here's an example of how to use the `KeepAlive` property in C# when working with the `HttpWebRequest` class:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        const string baseUrl = "http://example.com/api/";

        using (WebClient client = new WebClient())
        {
            client.KeepAlive = true;

            for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
            {
                string url = baseUrl + "route/" + i.ToString();
                Console.WriteLine($"Sending request to {url}");
                string responseText = client.DownloadString(url);
                Console.WriteLine($"Received response from {url}: {responseText}");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we create a new instance of `WebClient`, which is derived from `WebRequest`, and set its `KeepAlive` property to `true`. This behaviour is then applied to all requests made using the `DownloadString` method during the for loop.

